N2  - Oral e-Poster Presentations - Booth 2: Skull base 2 (Pituitary), October 14, 2024, 12:40 PM - 2:10 PM Background: In recent years, transsphenoidal surgery for the removal of intrasellar lesions has made significant progress, especially in the areas of endoscopic and microsurgical techniques. Both methods have their specific advantages: The endoscopic method is characterized by improved visualization of the surgical field, while the microsurgical technique is known for its high effectiveness and safety. Methods: In our study, 36 patients with various types of pituitary adenomas were treated: 14 with non-functioning, 9 with growth hormone-secreting, 4 with ACTH-secreting, 7 with recurrences of non-functioning adenomas, and 2 with other intrasellar lesions. Each patient initially underwent a microsurgical treatment, followed by an endoscopically-assisted inspection and evaluation using intraoperative 3-Tesla MRI. This multi-stage approach aimed to maximize the effectiveness of tumor resection while minimizing the risk to the patient. For the final assessment of the surgical outcome, all patients were examined neuro-endocrinologically, ophthalmologically, and via MRI 3 to 6 months after neurosurgical treatment. These comprehensive follow-up checks were crucial in evaluating the long-term results and quality of life of the patients’ post-surgery. Results: The use of intraoperative MRI was particularly advantageous in cases of recurrences of non-functioning pituitary adenomas. The endoscopically-assisted resection control showed benefits in detecting remaining tumor residues in growth hormone-secreting and non-functioning adenomas. In cases of ACTH-secreting adenomas or other intrasellar lesions (i.e. Rathke's cleft cyst), a purely microsurgical technique proved to be sufficient. These findings underline the importance of an individualized treatment approach for each patient. Conclusions: The results of this study mark a significant advance in the precise and effective treatment of sellar lesions. The combination of these technologies could increase the likelihood of complete resection and reduce the need for further surgeries. To validate the results and further substantiate the applicability of these combined approaches, it is necessary to increase the number of patients in future studies with a similar treatment setting.
