N2  - BACKGROUND: To validate the utility of 3-dimensional multimodal imaging (3D-MMI) and intraoperative navigation (ION) in endonasal endo-scopic surgery for non-functioning pituitary adenomas invading the cavernous sinus. MATERIAL AND METHODS: We performed 13 endonasal endoscopic operations for non-functioning pituitary adenomas invading the cavernous sinus(Knosp gradeI-II 5 cases, Knosp grade III-IV 8 cases) guided by 3D-MMI and ION techniques. The 3D-MMI and ION were used to identify the exact location of the carotid artery, then we operated through the postero-superior and/or anterolateralcorridor of the cavernous carotid artery (CCA) to remove pituitary adenomas invading the cavernous sinus. RESULTS: In all patients, the 3D-MMI and ION techniques enabled adequate visualization of the microsurgical skull base anatomy (bone, tumors, vascular structures) stereoscopically, especially the relationship between tumor and internal carotid artery (ICA). Near total resection (90-95% volume removed) in 2 cases, and gross total resection (>950% volume removed) in 11 cases. Pre-operative cranial neuropathies and visual field defects were improved in all patients. CONCLUSION: 3D-MMI and ION techniques were found to be helpful for endoscopic skull base surgery. It is convenient for doctors to locate important structures and lesions. allowing the surgeons to increase tumor resection and avoid ICA injury during endonasal endoscopic surgery for non-functioning pituitary adenomas invading the cavernous sinus.
