N2  - Introduction: Pituitary adenomas are common intracranial neoplasms, and nonfunctioning ones comprise a large subgroup of these adenomas. Complete resection is often difficult and may pose undue risk to neurologic and endocrine function. Radiosurgery plays an important role in the management of patients with nonfunctioning pituitary adenomas. This study examines the outcomes after radiosurgery in a large, multicenter patient population. Methods: Under the auspices of the North American Gamma Knife Consortium, 9 Gamma Knife radiosurgery (GKRS) centers combined their outcome data in 512 patients with nonfunctional pituitary adenomas. Prior resection was performed in 479 patients (93.7%) and prior fractionated external beam radiotherapy was performed in 34 patients (6.7%). Fifty-eight percent of patients had some degree of hypopituitarism prior to radiosurgery. Patients received a median dose of 16 Gy to the tumor margin. Median follow-up was 36 months (range 1 to 223 months). Results: Actuarial tumor control was 98%, 95%, 91%, and 85% at 3, 5, 8, and 10 years post-radiosurgery. Smaller adenoma volume (p=0.006) and absence of suprasellar extension (p=0.064) were associated with progression free tumor survival. New or worsened hypopituitarism after radiosurgery was noted in 21% of patients. History of prior radiation and higher tumor margin doses were predictive of new or worsening endocrinopathy following GKRS. New or progressive cranial nerve deficits were noted in 9% of patients. In multivariate analysis, decreasing age, increasing volume, history of prior radiation therapy, and history of prior pituitary axis deficiency were predictive or new or worsening cranial nerve function. Favorable outcomes of tumor control and neurological preservation were reflected in a 4 point radiosurgical pituitary score (RPS). Conclusion: GKRS is an effective management strategy for the majority patients with recurrent or residual nonfunctional pituitary adenomas. Delayed hypopituitarism is the most common complication. Neurological and cranial nerve function were preserved in more than 90% of patients. The RPS may predict outcomes for patients who undergo GKRS for a nonfunctioning adenoma.
