N2  - Objective: To evaluate the indication and efficiency of gamma knife radiosurgery for nonfunctioning pituitary adenoma. Methods: A total of 193 cases of nonfunctioning pituitary adenoma patients received gamma knife radiosurgery managed were followed up by a mean time of 46.2 months (from 36 months to 90 months). The patients were divided into gamma knife alone group(68 cases) and gamma knife combined with neurosurgery group(125 cases). The median tumor margin dose was 14.5 Gy. Results: In the 68 cases of γ-knife managed alone group, 46 patients (67.6%) showed tumor volume reduction, 19 patients (27.9%) showed no tumor volume change, and 3 patients (4.4%) showed tumor volume enlargement. The overall local control rate was 95.65% (65/68). Hypopituitarism appeared in 2 patients (2.9%) and vision impairment appeared in 2 patients (2.9%). In the 125 cases of patients with postoperative residue or recurrence, 81 patients (64.9%) showed tumor volume reduction, 35 patients (28.0%) showed no change in tumor volume, and 9 patients (7.2%) showed tumor volume enlargement. The overall local control rate was 92.8%(116/125). Hypopituitarism appeared in 7 patients(5.6%). No vision impairment was observed. The overall tumor control rate was 93.7%(181/193)with 9 cases (4.6%) of newly developed hypopituitarism. Conclusion: Gamma knife radiosurgery is an efficient and safe modality for nonfunctioning pituitary adenomas with few complications, especially for patients with tumor residue or recurrence after craniotomy.
