N2  - The history and technique of transsphenoidal operations for pituitary tumors are reviewed. Fifteen operations were performed for acromegaly, one for Cushing's disease, and the remaining thirty were nonfunctioning chromophobe adenomas. Tumor size with respect to suprasellar extension did not alter the surgical approach. Significant and immediate reduction in growth stimulating hormone levels were obtained in twelve cases out of fourteen. Marked improvement in the visual fields and cranial nerve palsies were obtained in large chromophobe tumors, and reduction in adrenocorticotrphic hormone (ACTH) below normal levels in one patient. Two episodes of meningitis occurred and no reoperations for cerebrospinal fluid rhinorrhea was necessary. There was no postoperative mortality and no worsening of neurologic status. On the basis of the above results and the comparative mortality and morbidity statistics of transfrontal surgery, transphenoidal removal of pituitary adenomas appears to be the preferred method of therapy in most cases.
